.Dd $Mdocdate: October 24 2026 $
.Dt CP 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cp
.Nd copy files and directories
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm cp
.Op Fl afipv
.Oo
.Fl R
.Op Fl H | L | P
.Oc
.Op Fl o Ar format
.Op Fl -dry-run
.Ar source target
.Nm cp
.Op Fl afipv
.Oo
.Fl R
.Op Fl H | L | P
.Oc
.Op Fl o Ar format
.Op Fl -dry-run
.Ar source ... directory
.Sh DESCRIPTION
In the first form,
.Nm
copies
.Ar source
to
.Ar target .
In the second form, where the last operand is an existing directory, each
.Ar source
is copied into that directory under its own name.
A source that is a directory is copied only with
.Fl R ,
with everything below it.
.Pp
Every copy
.Nm
makes, or refuses, is a record: by default nothing is printed and a refusal
goes to standard error; with
.Fl v
each copy prints
.Dq Ar source Li -> Ar target ;
with
.Fl o
the records are printed as a table, as JSON or YAML, or as the columns chosen,
in the common grammar of the EmiBSD programs.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a , Fl -archive
The same as
.Fl R p P :
the tree, links as links, times and modes kept.
.It Fl f , Fl -force
Remove an existing target before copying, whatever its permissions, without
asking.
Undoes an earlier
.Fl i .
.It Fl H
With
.Fl R ,
follow the symbolic links named on the command line; links found below are
copied as links.
.It Fl i , Fl -interactive
Before overwriting an existing target, ask
.Dq overwrite Ar target ?
on standard error and read one line from standard input; a reply starting with
.Sq y
or
.Sq Y
copies, anything else skips the file, which is not an error.
Undoes an earlier
.Fl f .
.It Fl L
With
.Fl R ,
follow every symbolic link.
.It Fl P
With
.Fl R ,
follow no symbolic link: every link is copied as a link.
This is the default with
.Fl R .
.It Fl p , Fl -preserve
Give the copy the source's access and modification times, mode, owner, group
and file flags, as far as permissions allow.
If the owner or group cannot be set, no error is reported and the set-user-ID,
set-group-ID and sticky bits are dropped from the copy's mode.
.It Fl R , Fl -recursive
Copy directories and everything below them.
Symbolic links are copied as links unless
.Fl H
or
.Fl L
says otherwise; FIFOs and device nodes are recreated rather than read.
Hard links are copied as separate files; to keep them use
.Xr pax 1
or
.Xr tar 1 .
.It Fl v , Fl -verbose
Print
.Dq Ar source Li -> Ar target
after each copy.
.It Fl o , Fl -output Ar format
Print every copy as a record:
.Cm name
(the target of each copy, one per line),
.Cm table
.Pq SOURCE TARGET TYPE SIZE ACTION ,
.Cm wide
(every field),
.Cm json ,
.Cm yaml
(a
.Li CopyList
document, see FILES), or
.Cm custom-columns=NAME:.field,...
for the fields chosen.
.It Fl -dry-run
Examine the sources and report every copy that would be made, as
.Fl v
or
.Fl o
asks, writing nothing.
.It Fl -no-headers
No header line on tables.
.It Fl -help
This description; with
.Fl o Cm json
or
.Fl o Cm yaml ,
as a record a tool can read.
.El
.Pp
Without
.Fl R
every symbolic link is followed, and
.Fl H ,
.Fl L
and
.Fl P
mean nothing.
Of
.Fl H ,
.Fl L
and
.Fl P ,
as of
.Fl f
and
.Fl i ,
the last one given wins.
.Pp
A target that already exists is written over and keeps its mode, owner and
group.
A target that does not exist gets the source's mode shaped by the
.Xr umask 2 ,
with the set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits dropped unless the copy is made by
the file's owner into the file's group and
.Fl p
is off.
A directory created by
.Fl R
gets the source directory's mode, unshaped by the umask, once its contents are
in place.
Runs of zero bytes in a file are written as holes, so a sparse source gives a
sparse copy.
.Pp
If a source and its target are the same file, the copy is refused.
A file is never copied over a directory, nor a directory over a file.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa bin/files/cp/schemas/copy.v1.json
The schema of the
.Li CopyList
document
.Fl o Cm json
and
.Fl o Cm yaml
print.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
Everything was copied.
.It 1
Something could not be copied; the rest was.
.It 2
The command line was wrong: an unknown option, a value of the wrong type, fewer
than two operands, or several sources with a target that is not a directory.
Nothing was copied.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A copy of
.Pa foo
named
.Pa bar :
.Pp
.Dl $ cp foo bar
.Pp
The text files into
.Pa /tmp :
.Pp
.Dl $ cp *.txt /tmp
.Pp
The directory
.Pa junk
and everything below it, as
.Pa /tmp/junk :
.Pp
.Dl $ cp -R junk /tmp
.Pp
An archive of a tree, links as links, times and modes kept:
.Pp
.Dl $ cp -a src dst
.Pp
What would be copied, as records, without writing a byte:
.Pp
.Dl $ cp -R --dry-run -o json src dst
.Pp
The copies that failed, from the records:
.Pp
.Dl $ cp -R -o json src dst | jq '.items[] | select(.action == \&"failed\&")'
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ls 1 ,
.Xr mv 1 ,
.Xr pax 1 ,
.Xr umask 2 ,
.Xr symlink 7
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility is compliant with the
.St -p1003.1-2008
specification.
The flags
.Fl a
and
.Fl v
are the extensions OpenBSD's
.Nm
has;
.Fl o ,
.Fl -dry-run ,
.Fl -no-headers
and the long forms of the flags are EmiBSD's.
The historic
.Fl r
is not accepted: it copied special files and links wrongly, and
.Fl R
does what it meant.
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v1 .
This one was written from scratch for EmiBSD in 2026, with OpenBSD's
.Nm
as the reference for its behaviour.
